## Why This Is Asked

A realistic problem tests memory caching patterns, closures, and promise coordination for network calls at scale.

## Key Concepts

- In-memory caching with Map keyed by URL and serialized options
- Closure-scoped caches per instance
- In-flight deduplication with a Promise map
- Cache invalidation via TTL and error handling (don’t cache failures)

## Code Example

```javascript
function createFetchCache(ttl = 5000) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inFlight = new Map();

  return async function fetchWithCache(url, opts = {}) {
    const key = url + JSON.stringify(opts);
    const now = Date.now();
    const cached = cache.get(key);
    if (cached && (now - cached.t) < ttl) return cached.v;

    if (inFlight.has(key)) return inFlight.get(key);

    const p = fetch(url, opts)
      .then(r => {
        if (!r.ok) throw new Error(r.statusText || r.status);
        return r.json().then(v => {
          cache.set(key, { t: Date.now(), v });
          return v;
        });
      });

    inFlight.set(key, p);
    try { return await p; } finally { inFlight.delete(key); }
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you add stale-while-revalidate behavior?
- How would you test for memory leaks and ensure TTL correctness?

Closures enable functional patterns: private state, currying, and memoization. Trade-offs: closures keep references alive, increasing memory footprint; avoid capturing huge objects. Edge cases: loops with var can close over the wrong index; use let. In UIs, closures can lead to stale state if components re-render without updating bindings; debouncing/memoization illustrate how closures bind behavior to a specific creation context. Debounce example:
```
const debounce = (fn, wait) => {
  let t;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(t);
    t = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), wait);
  };
};
```

